{"step_index":223,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T13:40:44Z","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Specificity**\n\nI'm focusing intently on tool specificity. I've internalized the instruction to avoid `cat` for file creation/appending – it's inefficient and I can replace the function more effectively. Now, I'm concentrating on internalizing the benefits of `grep_search` and other commands, especially to keep them separate from bash command usage. I'm exploring when and why it's truly essential to use grep in bash.\n\n\n**Refining Mobile Chart Usability**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on optimizing the Plotly chart for an iPhone 16 Pro, focusing on mobile-specific challenges. I've begun to enumerate and categorize all the UI aspects that hinder mobile usability, like the conflicting drag modes, legend size, intrusive mode bars, and inefficient margins, as well as inappropriate heights and clunky tooltips. I'm prioritizing adjustments to enhance touch interaction and overall mobile experience.\n\n\n**Improving Mobile Chart Layout**\n\nI'm making a concentrated effort to revise the chart layout for a tall, narrow screen, targeting about 393px width for an iPhone 16 Pro. I'm actively reducing margins, experimenting with legend positioning to prevent chart compression, and considering disabling fixed drag modes or enabling `fixedrange`. Height is being fine-tuned to around 350px. I'm now exploring how to configure `st.plotly_chart` to hide the mode bar and activate `scrollZoom`.\n\n\n**Analyzing Chart Components**\n\nI'm now analyzing the components of the interactive chart function to optimize its performance. Specifically, I'm examining the data selection (`.tail(90)`), the plotting of candlesticks and the EMA, support, and resistance lines. I intend to enhance clarity of the data shown and remove unnecessary lines. I want to make the function more efficient and flexible.\n\n\n**Adjusting Mobile Display**\n\nI'm now integrating crucial mobile-specific display adjustments to the chart. I've configured the `st.plotly_chart` calls to hide the mode bar and experimented with drag modes to fix scrolling issues on mobile devices. I'm considering using `dragmode=False` or `'pan'` to improve the user experience. I've also added changes to the legend layout.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"replace_file_content","args":{"AllowMultiple":false,"Description":"Optimize Plotly chart layout for mobile devices (iPhone 16 Pro).","EndLine":119,"Instruction":"Update plot_interactive_chart layout with horizontal legend and reduced margins.","ReplacementContent":"    fig.add_hline(y=entry, line_dash=\"solid\", line_color=\"blue\", annotation_text=f\"Entry: {entry:.2f}\", annotation_position=\"top right\")\n    fig.add_hline(y=tp, line_dash=\"solid\", line_color=\"green\", annotation_text=f\"TP: {tp:.2f}\", annotation_position=\"top right\")\n    fig.add_hline(y=cl, line_dash=\"solid\", line_color=\"red\", annotation_text=f\"CL: {cl:.2f}\", annotation_position=\"bottom right\")\n    \n    fig.update_layout(\n        title=dict(text=f\"Chart for {ticker_name}\", font=dict(size=14)),\n        xaxis_rangeslider_visible=False,\n        template='plotly_white',\n        margin=dict(t=60, b=10, l=10, r=50), # Tighter margins for mobile screens\n        height=350, # Slightly shorter to fit on iPhone screen without hiding the app\n        legend=dict(orientation=\"h\", yanchor=\"bottom\", y=1.02, xanchor=\"left\", x=0, font=dict(size=10)), # Horizontal legend on top\n        dragmode='pan' # Pan is much more mobile friendly than box select\n    )\n    return fig","StartLine":112,"TargetContent":"    fig.add_hline(y=entry, line_dash=\"solid\", line_color=\"blue\", annotation_text=f\"Entry: {entry:.2f}\")\n    fig.add_hline(y=tp, line_dash=\"solid\", line_color=\"green\", annotation_text=f\"TP: {tp:.2f}\")\n    fig.add_hline(y=cl, line_dash=\"solid\", line_color=\"red\", annotation_text=f\"CL: {cl:.2f}\")\n    \n    fig.updat